I didn't really intend to make a Valkyrie my first ascension, as I
purposely rotate through all the roles in turn, just to avoid a rut. I
do consistently get farther with samurai, monks, and valks, however,
so I suppose it's not too great a surprise.

A reasonably straightforward game. I always do the Mines before
Sokoban, which turned out well; a random housecat I tamed in Minetown
ate a chameleon corpse about five turns later, and Frankie the barbed
devil was born. All was well until he killed the only general store
shopkeeper in the entire dungeon (in Minetown), which made price
identification problematic. He also killed the (cross-aligned)
Minetown priest, so I didn't get my full set of protection until the
Valley of the Dead. Eventually, afraid he'd turn on me and I'd be
unable to handle him, I abandoned him in the Wine Cellars.

I ate my first tengu on Mines level eight, IIRC. It made me very
jumpy. I didn't manage to get teleport control until just before my
ascension run, and I had to go hang out in Sokoban and rest for more
than 20,000 turns to do that. I was teleporting randomly around levels
for most of the game, which was rather frustrating.

An altar on level five provided BUC checking as well as some holy
water for the bag of holding I found in the mines. Sokoban gave up an
amulet of reflection (one of the many, many sources of reflection I
found throughout the game), so I was a pretty happy camper. Slogging
downward through the dungeon, I got a bigroom, a couple of vaults, and
another altar/stash level just below the quest portal (which was on
Rogue). By this point I'd found a polished silver shield, another was
in Perseus' Statue, and I never got a single set of gray dragon scales
throughout the game, although I did end up with three or four sets of
silver dragon scales before I stopped keeping count.

The castle wand was a little hard to get to -- by the time I'd gotten
to the castle I was still wearing +1 dwarvish mithril, my +3 small
shield, and assorted mines-granted armor. There was a Titan in the
castle throne room, who proved difficult to carve my way to, and I
ended up leaping down into the Valley to escape and heal at one point.
I'd only found one ?oEA by this point (and no ?oEW) and no magic
markers, so my AC was fairly crappy. The castle wand gave me scrolls
of charging, gauntlets of power, speed boots, and a cloak of magic
resistance; I saved the last three charges for the very endgame.

The quest I did after the castle, and that was a cinch once I had the
proper gear. After that it became a bit of a blur; I'd finally found a
couple of magic markers, so my gear was all enchanted up, and nothing
could really stand in my way in a serious manner. I got Mjollnir
fairly late in the game, and only towards the end game got my skill up
to Expert. I spent most of the game using Sunsword as my main weapon
(at +0), just because I'd never found a magic lamp and I really liked
the light source.

Gehennom was actually sort of dull. Orcus brought in Yeenoghu on his
second turn, and Demogorgon on his fourth, so that was a bit of fun,
but otherwise it was a pretty quick trip down to the bottom.

I'd only found three wands of death by the time I was ready for my
ascension run, with eight charges between them. All of my scrolls of
charging were gone, as were my magic markers, largely on ?oGenocide
(one reverse-genocide of nurses to get my hit points up -- I only had
81 hits at level 12, hmm... and the rest to get rid of L and c. A
throne genocide did for green slimes, and I found two other genocide
scrolls, which did for h and R). If I'd been thinking clearly, I'd
have spent more time polypiling for wands and markers before the
ascension run, but I got overconfident and went ahead and did it.

Rodney died quickly, but ended up sucking up most of my /oDeath
charges; I saved the last two blasts for the Astral Plane, and only
had to take him down with normal combat the once. (Wasn't too bad.)
The high priest of Moloch was easy, although his little minion buddies
did more damage to me than anyone else before or since with their
wound spells. (I ended up using one of my ?oFH when I dropped below
200 HP, just to be safe.) The mysterious force was a pain in the
tuckus, although my intrinsic teleport control and umpteen bajillion
wands of teleport came in handy.

One last swing by the stash to grab all of my gems and most of my cash
(but not so much as to make me burdened), and I was off for the
Planes. Only to realize I'd only brought along three ?oGD, no markers,
and not enough wands of death. Oops. One wish for another wand of
death when Rodney promised to be a pain in the arse, and one for a
marker for that fourth ?oGD... and now my /oWish is at (1:0).

On the Astral Plane I decided I'd like to try Pestilence first, as I
was concerned about my /oDeath situation and didn't have enough (okay,
any) food on me. He and his priest buddies were a pain, but eventually
went down -- and it turned out to be a Chaotic altar (I was neutral).
So I sat in a corner repeatedly zapping my (1:0) wand of wishes (and
smacking Pestilence by hand, as I was out of /oDeath charges by now)
until I got one last wish, for a helm of opposite alignment.

Tra-la.

